<VI _name="lvai_af_remove_hook.vi" description="Deletes ONE top-level subVI node from an override (or any class member) in the ACTIVE PROJECT's application instance\2C then runs BD.Remove Bad Wires on it. A SERIES hook is BYPASSED first\3A for each pair Bypass Near[i] / Bypass Far[i] (the hook's input and output terminal names\2C e.g. `&lt;C&gt; in` / `&lt;C&gt; out`\2C `error in` / `error out`) the source end of the wire at Near (Is Source? TRUE) and every sink of the wire at Far are read\2C the Far wire is deleted\2C and the source is connected to each sink with Connect Wire - the inverse of lvai_af_add_hook's series insert. A pair whose terminal is not on the node is listed under Bypass Missing and never guessed. With no pairs the helper behaves as before. The node is the Occurrence-th (from 0\2C in SubVIs[] order) whose VI Name equals Hook VI Name exactly\3B Matches says how many there were\2C so a caller that read the diagram first with lvai_af_hook_read can refuse an ambiguous name before this runs. WHY REMOVE BAD WIRES IS NOT OPTIONAL\3A deleting a node that sat on a branched wire leaves a loose end\2C and the VI - and a sibling override of the same class - then reads state 0 with an error of type 1000088\3B Remove Bad Wires on the edited VI cleared both (the kit's pa_delete_subvi plus pa_clean_member route\2C measured). The kit's own delete removed EVERY node with a given terminal name\3B this one removes exactly one. Execution state before and after is read in this instance. It does NOT save\3A the caller saves in a separate run. UNMEASURED as a whole.">
